WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.630
SUDS NARASIMHAN: Hello, my
name is Suds Narasimhan.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:12.880
I'm a product manager in Google
Cloud for Cloud AI Notebooks.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.400
Today, I'm going to walk you
through an overview of Cloud AI

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.520
Notebooks our managed
JupyterLab notebook

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.520
service for enterprises
on Google Cloud.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.800
Today, we'll explore how
Cloud AI Notebooks can

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.740
help you explore data quickly
and develop an AI-enabled model

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.910
and deploy it into production.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.690
Artificial intelligence
and machine learning

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.440
are one of the most--

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.870
are one of the key
disruptive technologies

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.660
that enterprises have
encountered in the last four

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:41.980
or five years.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.090
And AI and ML will continue
to disrupt enterprises going

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.000
forward for the next 10 years.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.360
The key to unlocking value
with artificial intelligence

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.170
starts with a model.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.810
And the simplest way to develop
a model is to use notebooks

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.280
and, within notebooks, to
use open-source frameworks

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.640
to develop AI models.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.510
TensorFlow, PyTorch, and
many other OSS distributions

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:11.020
exist today that can help
you quickly develop a model

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.080
and gain insights.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.880
As, a data point, today,
more than 8 million Jupyter

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.010
notebooks run on GitHub.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:26.500
And this notebook count only
is going to continue to grow.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.680
Google has been in the
forefront of bringing notebooks

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.780
to different segments of
customers over the past decade.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.170
Kaggle, for example, is targeted
at enthusiasts and people

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.870
who are learning to
be data scientists.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.820
Colab is targeted at individual
researchers and students.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.990
And it provides a
collaborative environment

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:51.110
for developing data
science algorithms.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.250
AI Platform Notebooks are
targeted towards enterprises.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.560
This means that AI
Platform Notebooks

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.770
come with security built
in, which enterprises really

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.240
care about.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.050
Today, we're going to spend some
time looking at AI Platforms

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.970
and how it can speed up
your model development.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.940
So, as we noted,
enterprises want

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.160
insights in days, not weeks.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.500
This means that
data scientists want

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.710
to focus on the difficult
job of model development

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.750
and not, essentially,
the peripheral task that

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.300
goes around running a
notebook or the infrastructure

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.100
associated with a notebook.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.010
Enterprises encounter
several challenges

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.810
in running notebooks and
open-source frameworks.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.890
The first thing is around
managing open-source frameworks

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.055
and making these--

00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:37.930
making the operating
systems, the frameworks,

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.050
and the dependencies
work correctly.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.090
Hosting notebooks
and maintaining them

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:45.240
on top of these frameworks
is an additional pain point.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.240
Customers also want to
make sure that the access

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.520
to the data that
data scientists use

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.070
and the models developed
from the notebook themselves

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.800
are secure, and
they're only accessible

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.750
by the right personnel.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.020
Since AI and ML
involves accessing data

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.510
across the enterprise
landscape, it

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.550
is important for
a notebook service

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.950
to connect with all of
the data sources that

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.780
reside within an
enterprise's data estate.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.330
Our AI Platform Notebook
service solves these problems

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.150
by providing three key features.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.910
We completely manage
the installation

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.100
of the Jupyter
notebook environment,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.960
along with all the open-source
frameworks and dependencies.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.060
So you don't have to manage
any of that lifecycle.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.910
Our notebooks also ship
with security features

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:40.420
like VPC-SC, CMEK, and other GCP
horizontal features built in.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.440
And Cloud AI Notebooks is also
integrated with several data

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.830
services like
BigQuery and Dataproc

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:51.100
that we'll dive
into a little later.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.380
Notebooks are really
easy to get started.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:56.410
All you have to do is
go to the GCP console,

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.680
find Notebooks under the
AI Platform Notebook,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.920
create a new instance
with the required

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.230
deep-learning framework
and the type of VM

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:08.930
that you want to
run the notebook on.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:13.370
So what makes Cloud AI Notebooks
a fully managed experience?

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.740
What we do, as part
of Cloud AI Notebooks,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.649
is to pre-install all the
client libraries and all

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:19.860
the environments.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.550
We support popular
open-source environments

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:26.360
like TensorFlow, PyTorch,
and mathematical libraries

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.700
like NumPy and SciPy.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.740
To plot, we also provide
open-source tools

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.720
like Matplotlib built right
into our deep-learning VM.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.180
On top of these
deep-learning VMs,

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:41.000
our notebooks run, and
they take advantage

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.430
of these deep-learning
libraries.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.320
Customers can
configure the notebooks

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.610
through the Cloud console.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.740
However, they can
also get a link

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.980
to access the Jupyter
notebook using a proxy outside

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.370
of the GCP console.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.490
Cloud AI Notebooks
are designed to work

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:02.620
with different kinds of
compute instances within GCP.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.840
Cloud AI Notebooks can run with
DLVMs, as we just spoke about.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.510
These are GCE instances
that come pre-installed

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.850
with deep-learning frameworks.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.940
Cloud AI Notebooks can also
work with Dataproc clusters

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.040
and Dataflow.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.750
Cloud AI Platforms also
work with custom containers.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.230
So, if you have a specific
deep-learning library

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.810
that you want to run within
the custom container,

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.300
you can do so and
install a notebook

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.950
on top of these containers.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:31.450
We will talk a little bit more
about our Dataproc integration

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.350
in the slides that follow.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:37.610
Cloud AI Platform also works
with data sources within a GCP

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:38.720
like BigQuery.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.170
So you'll be able to query data
that's stored within BigQuery

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:45.640
and use that data to
train your models.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.620
Let's take a look at how
AI Platform Notebooks

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.210
are architected.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.860
AI Platform Notebooks
are designed

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:53.680
to work with a variety
of compute instances.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.260
We just talked about how it
works with deep-learning VMs,

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.290
which are GCE container--

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.740
GCE instances with
pre-installed deep-learning

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.102
frameworks within the VM.

00:06:05.102 --> 00:06:08.880
AI Platform Notebooks also
work with Dataproc clusters

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:09.980
and Dataflow.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:13.170
AI Platform Notebooks also
work with custom containers,

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:17.070
which means that, if you have a
special deep-learning framework

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.980
that you want to run, you can
create a custom container out

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.560
of it and have AI
Platform Notebooks work

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.020
with those containers.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:26.730
AI Platform Notebooks work
with a variety of data sources

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:27.930
within GCP.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:31.290
For example, you can
query data from BigQuery,

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.395
use that data as the
basis to train your model.

00:06:34.395 --> 00:06:36.270
AI Platform Notebooks
are also well

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.030
integrated with Git repositories
and other source control

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:39.810
systems.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:46.920
So it's easy to push and pull
code into these repositories.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.366
So why would you want to
use AI Platform Notebooks?

00:06:50.366 --> 00:06:52.470
AI Platform Notebooks
makes it really easy

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:55.440
to get started with
your model development.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.330
Since you don't have
to deal with installing

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.600
these deep-learning frameworks
or setting up the notebook

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:04.270
environment, you can get
started very, very quickly.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.670
In addition, since
AI Platform Notebooks

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.540
is a managed JupyterLab
service, the environment

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.850
around code development is very
familiar for data scientists.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:17.060
AI Platform Notebooks enable
you to scale and control costs

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:18.080
effectively.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.090
So you could start with
a very small instance

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:21.420
for model development.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.060
And, as you ramp
up your training,

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.100
you could move to a bigger VM.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:28.310
Or, better yet, you could use
a GPU or much powerful machines

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.640
in order to train your models.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.240
AI Platform Notebooks comes
with security built in.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.270
So, as we talked about VPC-SC,
IAM controls, as well as

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:43.050
CMEK controls are built right
into AI Platform Notebooks.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:46.110
AI Platform Notebooks are also
integrated with other services

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.220
within GCP.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:50.580
So it plays within an
ecosystem of analytic tools,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.250
as well as our AI Platform.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.380
So AI Platform
makes it really easy

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:59.270
to build, train, and
deploy these models.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.860
Let's talk about
some recent updates

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:02.840
that we have made to
our notebook service

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.340
in the last six months.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:08.870
We recently announced an
integration with Kaggle.

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:10.580
Kaggle, as you may
know, is a community

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:14.150
of 5 million data scientists
who write and share code.

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:17.270
With this integration between
Kaggle and AI Platform

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.570
Notebooks, customers
have access to GCP's

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:23.600
limitless and customizable
compute environment

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.990
that allows users to scale
up their work very easily.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.600
We've already seen thousands
of customers from Kaggle

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:33.320
go to Cloud AI Notebooks
using this integration,

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:37.220
and we expect these numbers to
grow in the next few months.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.140
One of the recent
integrations we announced

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:43.090
was with Dataproc through the
smart analytics framework.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:45.440
The smart analytics
framework brings together

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.750
Apache Beam and
Dataproc together

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:49.880
with AI Platform Notebooks.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:52.880
Customers typically need to
analyze petabytes of data

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.010
before starting to
work on a model.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:57.020
With this built-in
integration with Spark,

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:00.740
analytics and deep learning
can be authored from one place.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.530
The Dataproc Hub brings
enterprise controls

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:07.280
to Spark clusters based
on notebook interfaces.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.250
If you want to learn further
about Apache Beam notebooks,

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.430
there's a fantastic
tutorial when you launch--

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.050
when you launch a notebook
using smart analytics framework

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:17.900
and when you choose Apache Beam.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.540
As you can see, the
experience to using

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.630
smart analytics framework is
super easy to get started.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:27.290
All you would have to do is pick
the smart analytics frameworks

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:31.490
choice and either pick Apache
Beam or Dataproc distributions,

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:34.310
instead of a TensorFlow
or a PyTorch distribution,

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:37.830
as you would for a
deep-learning environment.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:39.710
So the goal of
Cloud AI Notebooks

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:43.340
is to help you develop models
quickly and allow you to easily

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:46.160
scale and streamline that
model within your production

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:47.660
workflows.

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:50.300
Let's take a quick look
at some of the examples

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:52.400
around how you can
go about doing that.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.150
Cloud AI Notebooks provides a
means to plug into your data

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.540
pipelines that enables
feature engineering

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.210
as well as data
labeling very easily.

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:05.450
Cloud AI Notebooks helps you
model your data efficiently

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:10.580
with what-if analysis and, also,
embedded AutoML capabilities.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:12.260
You can evaluate
your model quickly

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:14.840
with feature attributions,
what-if analysis,

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:16.850
and continuous evaluations.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:20.510
We've also built in algorithms
like optimization, AutoML,

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:23.540
and NAS right into
Cloud AI Notebooks

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:27.320
that makes model developments
simple and faster.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:29.480
On top of this,
Cloud AI Notebooks

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:32.690
runs on a hybrid platform and
a multi-cloud platform that

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:35.430
also has access to some of
the most powerful compute you

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:39.475
will find anywhere in the
world, for example, TPUs.

00:10:39.475 --> 00:10:43.250
Cloud AI Notebooks makes it easy
for you to scale and streamline

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:45.350
your model
development with ease.

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:49.010
Cloud AI Notebooks works in
an ecosystem of data services

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:51.080
and AI services.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.690
Cloud AI Notebooks can
be easily integrated

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:55.140
into your data pipeline.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.670
So, if you ingest
data using Data Fusion

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:02.480
or transform data using Dataproc
and put it into BigQuery,

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.360
you can easily access
that data, label the data,

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.430
and use that data for training.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.740
As previously mentioned,
Notebooks also

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:12.500
makes it easy to build,
develop, and train

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.600
a model with built-in tools such
as prebuilt algorithms, AutoML,

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:20.435
and access to our
training service.

00:11:20.435 --> 00:11:24.360
Cloud AI Notebooks also makes
it easy to deploy these models

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.370
by verifying your
model performance using

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.940
explainable AI, as well as
directly deploying the model

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:31.890
into our prediction service.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:33.420
Our prediction
service also ships

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.760
with a continuous
evaluation feature

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.450
that helps you to monitor the
model once it's in production.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:42.550
All these components
are built with access

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:44.440
to a pipeline that
makes it easier

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:49.640
to containerize and monitor
the entire workflow end to end.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.930
One such tool that's built
into AI Platform Notebooks

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.280
is Explainable AI.

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:57.190
Explainable AI is
a set of libraries

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.650
that help you figure
out why your model made

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:01.270
a particular prediction.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:05.620
For example, on the right, you
can see a color-coded heat map

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.770
of what the most important
features were in making

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:10.900
a particular prediction.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:15.400
Explainable AI can be useful
tabular, image, text TensorFlow

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.145
models.

00:12:17.145 --> 00:12:20.730
Explainable AI also ships
with a built-in What-If Tool.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:24.790
[INAUDIBLE] What-If Tool,
you could change the values

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:27.490
for attributes that you're
building with a model

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:30.100
and figure out how much
it impacts the prediction

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:33.550
of that particular model.

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:35.500
Explainable AI
libraries are already

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:37.750
available in some of our
deep-learning distributions

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:38.570
today.

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:42.760
And they will be available
in more in the coming weeks.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.280
Of course, AI Platform
Notebooks runs

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:49.030
in an AI-optimized
infrastructure within GCP.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:54.950
For example, our v3 TPU pods
provide 84% faster performance

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:57.850
in both object detection
and machine translation

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:01.210
compared to on-prem
infrastructure.

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:03.190
This AI-optimized
infrastructure is

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:07.420
only available on GCP
among public clouds.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:12.320
And they clearly outperform
on-prem systems in MLPerf.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:16.580
Looking ahead at our roadmap
extending beyond 2020,

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:20.360
it's worth looking back
on where we came from.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:22.460
We launched AI Platform
Notebooks in beta

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.190
in April 2019.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:25.940
And, since the time
that we have launched,

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:29.270
we've seen tremendous
interest among enterprises

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:32.600
for developing models
using Cloud AI Notebooks.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.640
We've seen significant
user growth and adoption

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.430
in the last year that
helped us successfully

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:40.190
GA the product in March 2020.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:43.130
Beyond 2020, we hope
to build in features

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:46.310
around machine learning
operations, lineage tracking,

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:49.640
collaboration, and integration
across our entire analytics

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:50.850
estate.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:54.680
As next steps, we invite you
to read our Notebooks Manifesto

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:57.950
blog that's available on
the Google Cloud blog site,

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:01.520
as well as get started with
Cloud AI Notebooks using

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:04.910
a five-minute tutorial that's
available on our product page.

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:07.280
You can also learn
about all the features

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:10.860
in in-depth documentation on
the product page on Google Cloud

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:12.718
Platform.

00:14:12.718 --> 00:14:14.010
Thank you for joining us today.

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:15.912
I hope you enjoyed
this presentation

00:14:15.912 --> 00:14:17.370
and you have a good
rest of the day

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:21.220
and you enjoy our other
Next '20 presentations.

